The differences, verified:

```js
// THIS — the headline difference
const obj = {
  name: 'Outer',
  regular() { return this.name; },   // this = the receiver (obj) → 'Outer' ✓
  arrow: () => this?.name,            // this = enclosing scope → undefined/global ✓
  delay() {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(this.name), 100);  // arrow INSIDE method:
  }                                    // captures the method's this → 'Outer' ✓
};
obj.regular();   // 'Outer'
obj.arrow();     // undefined (verified — no own this)
obj.delay();     // logs 'Outer' — the arrow kept the method's this
```

The mental model (the interview core): a regular function's `this` is decided AT CALL TIME — who called it (obj.method() → obj; plain call → undefined/global; new → the new instance). An arrow's `this` is decided AT WRITE TIME — it's the enclosing scope's this, permanently, exactly like a closed-over variable. That's why the setTimeout-in-a-method pattern works: the arrow inherits the method's this instead of losing it to the timer's call context.

Where each belongs (the practical answer):
- Arrow: callbacks that must KEEP the surrounding this (event handlers in class methods, timers, .map/.filter/.then continuations), and short pure transforms `x => x * 2`
- Regular: object methods (need dynamic this for chaining/receivers), anything used with new, functions needing arguments, and when you want hoisting (function declarations hoist; const arrows don't — TDZ)

The rest of the checklist: no arguments object in arrows (use `(...args) =>`), no prototype property, can't be called with new, implicit return only for a single expression (a block body needs explicit return).
